  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Appalachian Spindle is a small, delicate fungus belonging to the antler and spindle fungi family (Clavulinaceae), characterized by its slender, club-like fruiting bodies. This species is endemic to New England and represents an important part of the region's fungal diversity.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spindle-shaped fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Tall, narrow, and slightly flattened clubs that taper to a point, typically 2-5 cm in height",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pale coloration",
      "description": "Usually whitish, cream, or pale yellow in color with a smooth or slightly wrinkled surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth pattern",
      "description": "Grows singly or in small scattered groups among leaf litter and moss in woodland settings",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in moist, acidic forests and woodlands with rich organic matter, typically growing in mossy areas, leaf litter, and humus-rich soil throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other members of the Clavulinaceae family, the Appalachian Spindle likely forms beneficial relationships with plant roots, making it an important fungal partner in forest ecosystems.",
